==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|trifle}}, {{m|enm|trifel}}, {{m|enm|triful}}, {{m|enm|trefle}}, {{m|enm|truyfle}}, {{m|enm|trufful}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|trufle||mockery}}, a byform of {{m|fro|trufe}}, {{m|fro|truffe||deception}}, of uncertain origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtɹaɪfl̩/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-trifle.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪfəl|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|tri|fle|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[English]] [[dessert]] made from a [[mixture]] of thick [[custard]], [[fruit]], [[sponge cake]], [[jelly]] and [[whipped cream]].
# An [[insignificant]] [[amount]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1928|author=Lawrence R. Bourne
|title=Well Tackled!
|chapter=17|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5387037W
|passage=Commander Birch was a '''trifle''' uneasy when he found there was more than a popple on the sea; it was, in fact, distinctly choppy. Strictly speaking, he ought to have been following up the picket–boat, but he was satisfied that the circumstances were sufficiently urgent for him to take risks.}}
# Anything that is of little importance or worth.
#* Shakespeare
#*: '''Trifles''' light as air / Are to the jealous confirmation strong / As proofs of holy writ.
#* Drayton
#*: with such poor '''trifles''' playing
# A particular kind of [[pewter]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[utensil|Utensil]]s made from this particular kind of pewter.

